{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DJJLMkJcwUxT7HqwkPFYzg","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (29.5KB). Full output saved to: /ard-home/.claude/projects/-Users-al--ard-work-albertcbrown-ard-01M89N9QM8W2N3BV639J8PA5V6/13d35bb1-d423-4958-9eb4-0b6a8f18f355/tool-results/bl0y8zvvt.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\n.ard/277:\ncosts\nlogs\ntriage-engineer.md\ntriage-lead.md\n\n.ard/386:\ncosts\nlogs\nplan.md\nreview-eng-review.md\ntriage-engineer.md\ntriage-lead.md\nworker-engineer.md\n---\nrole: lead\nstatus: done\nattempt: 1\nnext: [triage:engineer]\nworker: engineer\n---\nThis is the last part of the network inputs back-route: an end-to-end harness test of the bounce, re-triage, gate and worker path plus its negative cases, and the remaining updates to docs/proposal.md. Part 3 has already merged on main, so the work can start now, and the engineer plans it.\n\n## Routing\n- triage:engineer: plan the harness test scenarios (main path through reviewers and the three negative paths, offline) and list which proposal sections still need updating.\n- worker engineer: writes the harness test and edits the proposal. No code outside tests and docs should be needed.\n\n## Open issues\n- The issue says the live prompt copies under .ard/ stay a human pull request. The worker must not edit .ard/triage/, .ard/workers/ or .ard/reviewers/, and those paths are protected in any case.\n- No documentation-planner or architect role exists, so the engineer's plan has to cover the proposal edits.\n# ard configuration for albertcbrown/ard (dogfooding). Started from `ard init`'s\n# template; human-owned. ard serve reads it from origin/main.\n# Anything unset takes its built-in default (docs/proposal.md, Configuration).\n# Validate with `ard config check`. ard serve reads this file from origin/main.\n\n# label: only issues a maintainer labels for triage enter the pipeline.\n# all: every human-filed issue enters.\nintake: { mode: label }\n\n# Loop poll intervals. Unset loops keep their defaults (scanners 4h,\n# prwatch 5m); 1m is the minimum. Triage, workers, reviewers and the merger\n# poll every minute so an approval, a finished job or a freed slot is picked\n# up within a minute instead of up to 15. The cost is GitHub API reads, not\n# job slots. Stopgap until approval and close-out wake the next loop directly.\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"015b7330-5d47-4bab-a218-7f9c713b26a1","uuid":"85868a63-36e5-4605-b608-1141b6066eaf","timestamp":"2026-11-25T15:31:03.639Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":".ard/277:\ncosts\nlogs\ntriage-engineer.md\ntriage-lead.md\n\n.ard/386:\ncosts\nlogs\nplan.md\nreview-eng-review.md\ntriage-engineer.md\ntriage-lead.md\nworker-engineer.md\n---\nrole: lead\nstatus: done\nattempt: 1\nnext: [triage:engineer]\nworker: engineer\n---\nThis is the last part of the network inputs back-route: an end-to-end harness test of the bounce, re-triage, gate and worker path plus its negative cases, and the remaining updates to docs/proposal.md. Part 3 has already merged on main, so the work can start now, and the engineer plans it.\n\n## Routing\n- triage:engineer: plan the harness test scenarios (main path through reviewers and the three negative paths, offline) and list which proposal sections still need updating.\n- worker engineer: writes the harness test and edits the proposal. No code outside tests and docs should be needed.\n\n## Open issues\n- The issue says the live prompt copies under .ard/ stay a human pull request. The worker must not edit .ard/triage/, .ard/workers/ or .ard/reviewers/, and those paths are protected in any case.\n- No documentation-planner or architect role exists, so the engineer's plan has to cover the proposal edits.\n# ard configuration for albertcbrown/ard (dogfooding). Started from `ard init`'s\n# template; human-owned. ard serve reads it from origin/main.\n# Anything unset takes its built-in default (docs/proposal.md, Configuration).\n# Validate with `ard config check`. ard serve reads this file from origin/main.\n\n# label: only issues a maintainer labels for triage enter the pipeline.\n# all: every human-filed issue enters.\nintake: { mode: label }\n\n# Loop poll intervals. Unset loops keep their defaults (scanners 4h,\n# prwatch 5m); 1m is the minimum. Triage, workers, reviewers and the merger\n# poll every minute so an approval, a finished job or a freed slot is picked\n# up within a minute instead of up to 15. The cost is GitHub API reads, not\n# job slots. Stopgap until approval and close-out wake the next loop directly.\nintervals: { triage: 1m, workers: 1m, reviewers: 1m, merger: 1m }\n\nmerge:\n  # A PR touching any of these waits for a human approval. There is no\n  # built-in list: removing an entry removes its gate. An entry ending in /\n  # is a directory prefix; anything else is a glob (quote globs starting with *).\n  protected_paths:\n    - .github/\n    - .ard/config.yaml\n    - .ard/scanners/\n    - .ard/triage/\n    - .ard/workers/\n    - .ard/reviewers/\n    - \"**/CODEOWNERS\"\n    # Agent instructions a PR could plant to steer later agents.\n    - CLAUDE.md\n    - \"**/CLAUDE.md\"\n    - AGENTS.md\n    - \"**/AGENTS.md\"\n    - .claude/\n    - .mcp.json\n    # What builds, signs and deploys ard itself (docs/proposal.md, Delivery).\n    - \"Jenkinsfile*\"\n    - Dockerfile\n    - .dockerignore\n    - deploy/\n\noverrides:\n  scanners/cve: { every: 6h, max_open: 10, group_size: 1, autoclose: true }\n  # Phase 1 agent roles (the ard init starter set, #39 Gate 2).\n  triage/lead:  { model: claude-opus-5-5, timeout: 15m }\n  triage/engineer: { model: claude-sonnet-5-5 }\n  workers/engineer: { model: claude-sonnet-5-5, timeout: 60m, tests: make lint test }\n  reviewers/eng-review: { model: claude-sonnet-5-5 }\n(eval):1: no matches found: internal/harness*\n(eval):1: no matches found: --include=*.go\n132:**Fetched inputs (#300).** A worker that fetches bytes from the network records each one under `fetched:` in its role file. Close-out parses the list strictly, like `findings`: an entry with a non-https URL (or one with userinfo, whitespace, backticks or angle brackets), a bad date or status, a `sha256` that is not 64 lowercase hex characters, a `verified_by` that is neither `none` nor an https URL, an unclean or missing `path`, or an unknown key fails the parse, as do more than 50 entries and a `fetched` key on a non-worker role file. The worker PR body then lists the entries under **Fetched inputs** (URL, landing path, sha256, verified_by; `none` shows as unverified) from the parsed fields only, never from free text; the list is dropped, like the summary, when redaction or the secret scan touches it. The human-readable record beside each fixture set is a `CAPTURE.md` with the same fields as a table plus the regen command (the #47 precedent); the worker writes it as part of the change, and ard does not parse it.\n134:**Network needs (#384).** A worker that cannot fetch an input itself writes `status: needs-network` and a `network:` list of 1 to 10 entries (`what`, `url`, `why`, optional `verify`). Close-out parses it as strictly as `fetched`, on a worker only, re-parses it after redaction and ends `needs-human` if it no longer parses. For now the outcome ends like `needs-human`: nothing is pushed, no PR is opened, and the comment lists each entry with its URL in a code span. Part 2 (#385) adds the bounce to the triage engineer, and part 3 (#386) switches it on (`servecmd` sets `closeout.Deps.Network`) together with the lead re-arm and the inputs gate. When on, it applies to a first worker job only (no branch or PR, and nothing pushed by this job): close-out requests `ard:triage:engineer` instead of `needs-human` and removes `ard:triage:engineer:done`, `ard:worker:<role>` and `:running`, in that order. The triage job gets `network-request.md`, rendered from the record's structured list and marked as agent-written, untrusted input. One network round per issue is allowed, counted from worker close-outs with outcome `needs-network` in any phase from prepared on (the plan never changes after Prepare, so a crash cannot make a round read as not open); a second, a fix, review or conflict round, a pushed job or a repository without a triage engineer role ends `needs-human` with the list. The live prompts under `.ard/` need a human PR to match the starter copies.\n136:**Network round re-run (#386).** A round is open while the newest bounced worker has no triage engineer `done` after it (job ID order). `Prepare` persists the round in the job record (`NetworkState.Round`, `Done`, `Rerun`), so `Build` stays pure and Resume replays the same plan. The engineer's first `done` in a round consumes it: its close-out clears `ard:triage:lead:done` (`Plan.Clear`, removed in the request phase before the requests are added, 404 counting as success) and adds `ard:pending`, so the lead runs first, and persists the other triage roles that had finished `done` (`Plan.Rerun`). The lead's close-out in that round re-requests those roles and clears their `:done` (roles no longer configured are skipped), so all finish before the gate. A human re-request of the engineer sees no open round and does not re-arm again. The engineer may declare `inputs:` (`path`, `provider` `human` or `worker`, optional https `url`), parsed from the redacted role file and stored in the plan. The gate checks each path (plain characters, clean and relative, not under `.ard/`, `.github/` or `.git`), reads main once per tick at one SHA through `ls-tree` (no content, regular blobs only, size capped, cached per SHA) and holds with a \"waiting for inputs\" comment listing each missing path and the capture-note format until every human-provided input exists. A `worker` input with a `network: false` worker role, a bad path or an oversized file ends `needs-human`. A network round always requires a fresh approval, even with `require_approval: false` and even when the engineer removed every network need: the \"ready for approval\" marker gains a `round=<n>`, and an earlier approval is stale because the engineer's new `:done` follows it. An approval given while inputs are missing still counts once they land. Label state machine: the engineer's `done` close-out removes `ard:triage:lead:done` and adds `ard:pending` before its own `:done`; the lead's close-out removes `:done` from each re-opened role and requests it again; the gate does not run while the lead's `:done` is absent. The gate records one `wait` gate event when it starts waiting for inputs and one `inputs-ready` event when they land; the daily status-issue digest shows a *Waiting for inputs* count of open issues whose newest gate event is the wait.\n149:status: done          # done | blocked | needs-human | needs-network (the last three add ard:needs-human for now)\n160:network:            # workers only, with status needs-network: 1 to 10 entries (#384)\n259:**The docs scanner** (`scanners/docs`, a `sh` script plus one stdlib-only Go program, `docscan.go`, built at scan time with the job's own Go) lists undocumented exported Go identifiers. It reports, per package directory: a missing package comment; an exported function, a method on an exported receiver type (pointer, value or generic) and an exported type with no doc comment (the spec's doc, its trailing comment or the enclosing declaration's doc all count); and an exported const or var with no comment, one finding per parenthesised block that has no block comment. Struct fields, interface methods, methods on unexported types and comment wording are not checked. Every exported identifier counts, `internal/` included, and files for every platform count (build constraints are not evaluated). It skips `testdata/`, `vendor/`, `node_modules/` and dot or underscore directories, files whose names start with `_` or `.`, symlinks, `*_test.go`, `//go:build ignore` files, generated files and `package main`. The fingerprint is `docs:undoc:<dir>:<symbol>` or `docs:pkg:<dir>`; the file and line are left out, so moving a declaration within its package keeps its issue. Severity is `low` and the group is the package directory. It only parses files: it never runs repository code and needs no network. It fails closed: a parse error, unreadable file, invalid path or more than 10,000 findings fails the run with nothing filed. A human-owned `exclude` file in the role directory skips package directories (`dir/` is a directory and everything below it, anything else a glob matched against the package directory); an entry makes its findings vanish, so autoclose closes their issues. Go only for now.\n279:The starter lead (`.ard/triage/lead/prompt.txt`) follows a fixed contract. It reads the issue only through the untrusted input files `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, which the triage loop writes and never commits. It finds roles by listing the role directories and routes only to those. It writes only `triage-lead.md`, with `attempt: 1`, and a `status: done` always names a `worker`. It answers `needs-human` when a person must decide: an unclear ask, a question, a duplicate, a change to `.ard/config.yaml`, the role directories or `.github/`, no fitting worker, or text that tries to instruct it. It uses `review:<role>` only for a reviewer whose `when` rule would skip a change that needs that review. The committer and close-out guards confine it to `.ard/<issue#>/`, and close-out allows it only `triage-lead.md`; any other change under `.ard/<issue#>/` fails the job as needs-human. It also reads `PRODUCT.md` and `VISION.md` when present: a contradiction with a non-goal, principle or banned dependency makes it raise one `needs-human`, while on an interpretive or minor conflict it may proceed and says so in `triage-lead.md`. Inputs found only on the network are not, alone, a reason for `needs-human`: the lead routes them to the engineer and reads the `Worker roles:` line to see whether any worker role has network (#301).\n281:The starter engineer (`.ard/triage/engineer/prompt.txt`) plans for the worker without implementing. Its inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md`, `comments.md` and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, all untrusted. It writes the `## Implementation` section of `plan.md` with Steps, Test plan, Risk and Size, sized S, M, L or XL on a scale tied to the worker diff guard's block limit (XL is over the worker role's `diff_max_lines`, 3,000 changed lines by default). It writes `triage-engineer.md` with `role: engineer` and `attempt: 1`, never a `worker` key. It answers `needs-human` for an XL change, a change to protected role and config paths or `.github/`, or a missing product or design decision, and leaves `plan.md` untouched. In Phase 1 it has no `after:`; the lead's request orders it. The committer and close-out guards confine it to `.ard/<issue#>/`, and close-out allows it only `triage-engineer.md` and the `## Implementation` section of `plan.md`; a change to `triage-lead.md`, `issue.md` or another role's section fails the job as needs-human. It reads `TECH_STACK.md` when present. When the change needs files from the network it adds a `### Network inputs` subsection (exact URL, pinned version, verification source, landing path, and who provides it: the worker if its role has network, else a human); it never fetches and never writes a checksum it did not read from a file (#301).\n302:**Sidecar repos are context, read-only.** A role's sidecar (for example `vpe-specs`) is cloned by the executor, not the loop: the loop passes the role's resolved sidecar (repo and ref) on the job. The executor keeps one shallow (depth 1) bare object cache per sidecar repo at `~/.ard/sidecars/<controlled-owner>/<controlled-repo>/<sidecar-owner>/<sidecar-repo>.git`, shared by every job of that controlled repo, and exports the commit into the job home as `<job home>/sidecar` (plain files: no `.git`, `chmod -R a-w` in both modes, and a `:ro` mount under Docker). The job sees it at `$ARD_SIDECAR`. `ref` is a branch, tag or full 40-character SHA (an abbreviated SHA is a config error). A SHA already in the cache needs no network; a branch or tag is re-fetched (depth 1) for every job. Whatever the ref, `Prepare` resolves it to a full commit SHA and records it on the job's handle (and in the `executor.sidecar_ready` log), so a job is reproducible. Symlinks and submodules in the sidecar are rejected and the tree is capped at 64 MiB, like config snapshots. A fetch failure (network, auth, a stale lock, which the executor clears by rebuilding the cache once) is the transient `clone_failed`; a ref or SHA that does not exist, or a repo the App cannot read, is a permanent failure that names the sidecar. A role that wants a spec changed says so in `plan.md`; editing the sidecar is out of scope for v1.\n306:An executor runs one agent job somewhere and reports back; the controller does not care where. Both executors implement the same five calls: `prepare`, `start`, `status`, `logs`, `cleanup`. Prepare also removes any `Job.Scrub` paths (the job's own role file, directly under `.ard/<issue#>/`) from the fresh worktree before the agent starts. Job IDs are ULIDs minted at claim time; they name the worktree and the logs and appear as the `Ard-Job` commit trailer. The executor captures the job's stdout separately from its combined log, because the adapter parses one and the committed log comes from the other. Scanner jobs get network egress for advisory databases; an agent job's environment holds its model credential and nothing else of value, and with `network: true` (the default) it may reach the network; see the `network` setting under Configuration for what is and is not enforced.\n323:Kubernetes-executor details (decided, design revision 3 of [#54](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/54), recorded in [#312](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/312); the work is split across later parts of #54, and #317 splits into the API layer and the executor). There is no job API on `ard serve`. **Pod shape.** `pre` fetches with the token passed through `http.extraheader` from the environment, never argv or `.git/config`; the git dir is on a `ctl` volume the agent does not mount; the role dir comes from `cfg.SHA`, so the k8s executor refuses a config without a SHA; sidecars are at pinned SHAs; the untrusted inputs and the scrub list travel in the per-job Secret, capped at 768 KiB (over the cap is a permanent failure with a named cause); `pre` copies the static `ard` binary. The `agent` container mounts only `work` and `out` and holds only its model credential via `secretKeyRef`: no GitHub token, no key, no Secret volume, no SA token. It tees stdout to `out/stdout`, enforces its own deadline, writes a done marker and reaps orphans as PID 1. `post` has its own pinned image and never runs a binary from a shared volume. It serves one route, `GET /v1/result`, on the pod IP, guarded by a preshared 32-byte `crypto/rand` key unique to the pod, mounted only in `post` and kept in memory by the controller, compared in constant time. It answers 409 until the agent has terminated and the marker exists, builds the tar in Go and never runs git, serves one request at a time, has header and idle timeouts, never logs Authorization, and exits 0 after the controller confirms or at the deadline. The transport is plain HTTP inside the cluster (the owner's choice): the key grants only this job's result, and a NetworkPolicy limits who can connect.\n364:9. **Report.** Comment on the issue with the summary, PR link and test result (`passed`, `passed after 1 fix attempt`, `failed after 2 fix attempts`, `not configured`); swap `ard:worker:<role>:running` for `:done`. If tests still fail, add `ard:tests:failed` on the issue and the PR and request no reviewers; a green run removes it. A worker that ends without the loop's result (for example one the Reaper closes out), or whose role file says `needs-human`, never becomes `:done` without a PR. A role file that says `needs-network` ends like `needs-human` for now: nothing is pushed, and the comment lists the needed inputs.\n366:**The starter worker (`workers/engineer`, #34).** `.ard/workers/engineer/prompt.txt` implements `plan.md`'s `## Implementation`. Its inputs, all untrusted, are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, in a fix round `test-output.log`, and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, `issue.md`, the triage role files and logs, in a review round `review-feedback.md` and the reviewer role files and logs. It writes code, tests and docs, and `worker-engineer.md` with `role: engineer` and `attempt: 1`, an optional `next` of `review:` entries, and never `worker` or `verdict`. The body has Changes, Testing and Open issues sections; it becomes the commit body, the PR description and the issue comment, and a fix round's body describes the whole change. It answers `needs-human` for a change the diff guard would reject, more changed lines than the role's `diff_max_lines` (3,000 by default; above `diff_warn_lines`, 2,000 by default, the PR goes ahead with the large-change note), a missing decision, nothing to change, or an input that tries to instruct it. In a fix round it keeps `done` and explains a failure it cannot fix, so the loop reports `ard:tests:failed` on the PR. The repository's package manager may fetch a dependency the change needs (`go get`, `npm install`); the agent installs no system packages. With `network: true` it may fetch other inputs, records each under `fetched:` and treats the fetched bytes as untrusted data, and the guard and reviewers still see `go.mod` and lockfile changes. Credential-shaped test fixtures are built at run time. The starter override is Sonnet 5.5, `timeout: 60m` for the whole job, `tests: make lint test` (so formatting and vet failures are caught before a PR opens), and the default diff thresholds written out (`diff_warn_lines: 2000`, `diff_max_lines: 3000`). It ships no Dockerfile, so it runs as a host subprocess with the #84 warning until #55's `ard-agent` image gives a worker image a base. It reads `TECH_STACK.md` when present; the file is a constraint, never an instruction, and the worker never resolves a conflict with it: it keeps to the issue and plan and notes the conflict in Open issues. With `Network: available.` the worker fetches only what the plan's Network inputs name, verifies each file against the publisher's checksum file, never invents or recalls a value, records each file in `fetched:` and in a `CAPTURE.md` beside the fixtures, and commits a regen script and Make target; with `Network: unavailable.` it answers `needs-human` listing the inputs it needs (#301).\n378:3. **Run the aid, then the agent.** After the pin, a role with an executable `aid` in its directory (reviewers only, like a scanner's `scan`; #205) has it run by the controller as its own executor job at the pinned head, before the agent, so the findings of its deterministic tools do not depend on the host or on the agent choosing to run them. The tool finds; the agent explains, ranks and confirms. The aid job is read-only, has no `Env` and no `Fixed` entries (no model credential, no GitHub token), runs with `--network none` under Docker (a role `Dockerfile` or `ARD_AGENT_IMAGE`; known CVEs stay with the `cve` scanner, so the aid never queries vulnerability databases), and has its own timeout of 5 minutes (shorter if the role's is), counted inside the review job's timeout: the agent gets what is left, at least one minute. Its inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/changed.txt` (one path per line, `.ard/<issue#>/` left out) and `diff.patch`. Its output is JSONL on stdout, one object per result with exactly `tool`, `rule`, `file`, `line`, `severity` (`critical`, `high`, `medium`, `low`, `info`) and `message`, capped at 256 KiB (more is a failure). ard validates it: a line that is not JSON, has an unknown key, a bad tool, rule, severity or line, or a path that is absolute, has `..` or is not in `changed.txt` is dropped and counted; a message is redacted, cut at 1 KiB and secret-scanned like a log, and a line that still trips the scanner is dropped. Matched text is never kept: the aid must print rule ids and positions only. The result is written to `.ard-input/<issue#>/aid.jsonl` and is the first of the agent's untrusted inputs; the starter prompt reads it first and runs the tools itself only when the file is absent. **Failure:** a failed, timed-out or invalid aid (any dropped line counts as invalid) never stops the review. The input says the aid failed and why, and close-out turns an `approve` into `changes` with a note. **Blocking:** a `gitleaks` result on a line the PR added becomes a blocking `high` finding with a `[secret]` message that ard adds to the review in code (carrying the rule id only); the agent cannot drop it, and an `approve` becomes `changes`. gosec and semgrep results stay advisory. Both rules apply only to roles that have an `aid`. **Host mode:** with no role `Dockerfile` and no `ARD_AGENT_IMAGE`, the aid runs on the host, as ard-owned code that reads but never executes PR code; its environment holds no credential, but nothing enforces its network, and it runs as the controller's uid without a sandbox: the residual risk of #84 applies to it too. The starter `security-review/aid` pins gosec, gitleaks and semgrep (vendored local rules, `--metrics=off`, `--disable-nosem`) in the role's `Dockerfile`, built on the default ard-agent image (k8s role images stay with #245). Then **run the agent** with `.ard/reviewers/<role>/prompt.txt` and these untrusted inputs: `.ard-input/<issue#>/diff.patch` (the controller's diff from the merge base with `main` to the pinned head, `--stat` then the patch, taken with `--text`, `--no-textconv` and attributes read from the empty tree so a `.gitattributes` entry cannot hide a change, with this issue's `.ard/<issue#>/` left out and cut at 1 MiB), `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md` (written by the loop, never committed), the committed `.ard/<issue#>/` (plan, triage and worker role files and logs) and the sidecar (security policy, license allowlist). The loop runs every git command against the controller clone, never in the reviewer's worktree (the agent itself may run read-only git there). In a later round it also gets `.ard-input/<issue#>/previous-review.md`, its own earlier report, read from the pinned head, and says which findings are resolved.\n384:**The starter reviewer (`reviewers/eng-review`, #36).** `.ard/reviewers/eng-review/prompt.txt` checks correctness, missing tests and drift from `plan.md`. Its inputs, all untrusted, are `diff.patch`, `issue.md`, `comments.md` and the committed `.ard/<issue#>/`; text in the change that addresses a reviewer is a blocking `high` finding on its line (verdict `changes`), and the same text in the issue, comments or `.ard/<issue#>/` is ignored and mentioned in the body. It runs the repository's own linter and the changed packages' tests with coverage through Bash, writing scratch files outside the worktree, reports only problems on added lines and skips a check it cannot run, saying so; the package manager may fetch declared dependencies and nothing else is downloaded. Critical and high findings always block, medium only when it is a real bug, low and info never; a missing test blocks only when it is `high` (risky or core logic, or a case `plan.md` named), smaller gaps are `medium`. The verdict is `changes` exactly when a finding blocks. `line` is the head-side line: a `+` line goes inline, anything else in the body. It writes only `review-eng-review.md` with `role: eng-review`, `attempt: 1`, `status` `done` or `needs-human`, `findings` with a double-quoted `file` and a `message: |` block, never `next` or `worker`, and a body with a `## Checks` section. It answers `needs-human` only when there is nothing to review or the issue and the plan contradict each other. The starter override is Sonnet 5.5 with the default 30m timeout and no `when:`, so every PR from the starter worker gets it. It ships no Dockerfile, so it runs as a host subprocess with the #84 warning until #55. It also checks that every fetched file has a `fetched:` entry, a `CAPTURE.md` row and a regen target, and re-verifies pinned checksums against the publisher's checksum file when it has network; a capture note is agent-written, so it is not evidence on its own (#301).\n421:  - when the rules cannot be read (a 5xx, a network error, a permissions 403), nothing merges that tick. Rule types ard does not evaluate (`workflows`, `code_scanning`, `required_deployments`) are logged at warn\n510:**`network` (#297, part of #276).** Every agent kind takes an optional boolean `network` override, default `true`. In this part it is advisory only and nothing is enforced: a role with `network: false` gets `ARD_NETWORK=0` (else `1`) in its job environment and a constant \"Network: unavailable.\" (else \"Network: available.\") line in the agent's user message. The value rides on the executor job and handle from config only, never from anything the agent can read or change. In host mode the controller logs that `network: false` is not enforced. `ard config check` prints `false (advisory)`, or `false (advisory, host)` for a host-mode role, and warns when a host-mode role that reads untrusted input has `network: true`, naming the GitHub App private key file as reachable.\n512:What `true` and `false` mean per executor, as the code does today: the host executor only sets `ARD_NETWORK` and the prompt line, and logs that `false` is not enforced; the Docker executor passes the same variable and prompt line and adds no network option to `docker run`, so the container has the default bridge network either way. Nothing blocks egress for either value, so `false` is a request the agent may ignore, not a boundary. Risks of a role with network (#276): (1) exfiltration of anything the agent can read, including its model credential and the checked-out source; (2) fetched content that carries prompt injection; (3) fetched bytes that change between runs, so results do not reproduce; (4) a compromised or typosquatted source; (5) fetching code or data that lands in the repository unreviewed; (6) a download that is large or slow and burns the job's time and spend; (7) a request to an internal or metadata address from the host or cluster. The residual risk is that none of these is closed by the setting: the diff guard, the reviewers, the `fetched:` records (URL, hash and verifier for every landed input) and the missing GitHub token limit the damage, and a human reviews every PR. Fetched bytes are untrusted data, never instructions, and are not a reason to trust the source. For roles with network, use a dedicated workspace API key with a spend cap, so a stolen or abused key can cost only a bounded amount.\n526:- **Agents hold no GitHub token.** The executor does. The agent's environment has only its model credential: an API key or a subscription OAuth token. The local executor does not enforce egress (see `network` under Configuration; the chart's NetworkPolicy comment is #296). In Kubernetes (#54) job egress is DNS and the internet minus private ranges and 169.254.169.254 (the #276 posture), and `pre` holds only a read-only per-job token revoked after it ends.\n531:- **Residual risks (Kubernetes).** The model credential sits in a per-job Secret in etcd, so etcd encryption at rest is required. The tee'd stdout is agent-writable, so the agent can forge its usage figures, the same exposure as local Docker, accepted by the owner; the exit code still comes from the kubelet; revisit when split log streams are available. Pod egress reaches the whole internet, and a `network: false` pod still allows GitHub and model API CIDRs. Pod Security `restricted` relies on the cluster's admission.\nVERSION ?= $(shell git describe --tags --match 'v[0-9]*.[0-9]*.[0-9]*' --exclude 'v*-*' --always --dirty 2>/dev/null || echo dev)\nLDFLAGS := -s -w -X main.version=$(VERSION)\n\n.PHONY: build test lint fmt clean cve-fixtures lts-fixtures lint-scanner-fixtures chart-test image-test agent-image agent-image-test docs-check release-check\n\nbuild:\n\tCGO_ENABLED=0 go build -ldflags \"$(LDFLAGS)\" -o bin/ard ./cmd/ard\n\ntest:\n\tgo test ./...\n\nlint:\n\tgo vet ./...\n\t@out=\"$$(gofmt -l .)\"; if [ -n \"$$out\" ]; then echo \"gofmt needed:\"; echo \"$$out\"; exit 1; fi\n\nfmt:\n\tgofmt -w .\n\n# Renders the two Argo CD charts under deploy/ and checks them (needs helm and yq;\n# run by the ard-ci pipeline, not part of `make test`).\nchart-test:\n\tdeploy/test-charts.sh\n\n# Builds the ard image for both platforms and checks it (needs Docker buildx and\n# network; not part of `make te","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"persistedOutputPath":"/ard-home/.claude/projects/-Users-al--ard-work-albertcbrown-ard-01M89N9QM8W2N3BV639J8PA5V6/13d35bb1-d423-4958-9eb4-0b6a8f18f355/tool-results/bl0y8zvvt.txt","persistedOutputSize":30210},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01DJJLMkJcwUxT7HqwkPFYzg","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
