{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","content":"Exit code 1\n---\nrole: engineer\nstatus: needs-human\nattempt: 1\n---\nThe request spans role-file parsing, close-out outcomes and label phases, the triage input file, lead re-arm with persisted round state, a gate inputs check with approval changes, a loop guard, starter prompts, an end-to-end harness test and proposal updates. Counting tests and docs, that exceeds one reviewable worker change (XL), so no plan was written and a split is proposed.\n\n## Open issues\n- Proposed split, in order:\n  1. Worker signal: add the `needs-network` status and `network:` list to `internal/closeout/rolefile.go`, parse into `Plan.Network` after redaction in `prepare.go`, add `OutcomeNeedsNetwork` in `plan.go` (`Build`, including the Reaper case) and render the comment. In this part the outcome ends `needs-human` with the list in the comment, and nothing is pushed.\n  2. Bounce: the `closeout.NetworkRound` helper, the label request and remove phases in `closeout.go` (modelled on `LeadWorker`), the `network-request.md` triage input, the one-round loop guard, the branch-exists and already-pushed checks, and the starter engineer and worker prompt copies in `internal/roles/`.\n  3. Re-run and gate: lead re-arm and persisted round state in the record, the re-run list for other triage roles, the `inputs:` frontmatter check and *waiting for inputs* status in `internal/triage/fanin.go`, and the per-round approval marker with forced approval.\n  4. The end-to-end harness test and proposal updates.\n- The lead also noted that no architect role is available. A maintainer may want to confirm the persisted round-state design before part 3.\n- Live prompts under `.ard/workers/` and `.ard/triage/` are protected and need a human PR.\n21:  protected_paths:\n22-    - .github/\n23-    - .ard/config.yaml\n24-    - .ard/scanners/\n25-    - .ard/triage/\n26-    - .ard/workers/\n27-    - .ard/reviewers/\n28-    - \"**/CODEOWNERS\"\n29-    # Agent instructions a PR could plant to steer later agents.\n30-    - CLAUDE.md\n31-    - \"**/CLAUDE.md\"\n32-    - AGENTS.md\n33-    - \"**/AGENTS.md\"\n(eval):1: no matches found: --include=*.go\n132:**Fetched inputs (#300).** A worker that fetches bytes from the network records each one under `fetched:` in its role file. Close-out parses the list strictly, like `findings`: an entry with a non-https URL (or one with userinfo, whitespace, backticks or angle brackets), a bad date or status, a `sha256` that is not 64 lowercase hex characters, a `verified_by` that is neither `none` nor an https URL, an unclean or missing `path`, or an unknown key fails the parse, as do more than 50 entries and a `fetched` key on a non-worker role file. The worker PR body then lists the entries under **Fetched inputs** (URL, landing path, sha256, verified_by; `none` shows as unverified) from the parsed fields only, never from free text; the list is dropped, like the summary, when redaction or the secret scan touches it. The human-readable record beside each fixture set is a `CAPTURE.md` with the same fields as a table plus the regen command (the #47 precedent); the worker writes it as part of the change, and ard does not parse it.\n134:**Network needs (#384).** A worker that cannot fetch an input itself writes `status: needs-network` and a `network:` list of 1 to 10 entries (`what`, `url`, `why`, optional `verify`). Close-out parses it as strictly as `fetched`, on a worker only, re-parses it after redaction and ends `needs-human` if it no longer parses. For now the outcome ends like `needs-human`: nothing is pushed, no PR is opened, and the comment lists each entry with its URL in a code span. Part 2 (#385) adds the bounce to the triage engineer, and part 3 (#386) switches it on (`servecmd` sets `closeout.Deps.Network`) together with the lead re-arm and the inputs gate. When on, it applies to a first worker job only (no branch or PR, and nothing pushed by this job): close-out requests `ard:triage:engineer` instead of `needs-human` and removes `ard:triage:engineer:done`, `ard:worker:<role>` and `:running`, in that order. The triage job gets `network-request.md`, rendered from the record's structured list and marked as agent-written, untrusted input. One network round per issue is allowed, counted from worker close-outs with outcome `needs-network` in any phase from prepared on (the plan never changes after Prepare, so a crash cannot make a round read as not open); a second, a fix, review or conflict round, a pushed job or a repository without a triage engineer role ends `needs-human` with the list. The live prompts under `.ard/` need a human PR to match the starter copies.\n136:**Network round re-run (#386).** A round is open while the newest bounced worker has no triage engineer `done` after it (job ID order). `Prepare` persists the round in the job record (`NetworkState.Round`, `Done`, `Rerun`), so `Build` stays pure and Resume replays the same plan. The engineer's first `done` in a round consumes it: its close-out clears `ard:triage:lead:done` (`Plan.Clear`, rem\n\n... [18403 characters truncated] ...\n\nd high findings always block, medium only when it is a real bug, low and info never; a missing test blocks only when it is `high` (risky or core logic, or a case `plan.md` named), smaller gaps are `medium`. The verdict is `changes` exactly when a finding blocks. `line` is the head-side line: a `+` line goes inline, anything else in the body. It writes only `review-eng-review.md` with `role: eng-review`, `attempt: 1`, `status` `done` or `needs-human`, `findings` with a double-quoted `file` and a `message: |` block, never `next` or `worker`, and a body with a `## Checks` section. It answers `needs-human` only when there is nothing to review or the issue and the plan contradict each other. The starter override is Sonnet 5.5 with the default 30m timeout and no `when:`, so every PR from the starter worker gets it. It ships no Dockerfile, so it runs as a host subprocess with the #84 warning until #55. It also checks that every fetched file has a `fetched:` entry, a `CAPTURE.md` row and a regen target, and re-verifies pinned checksums against the publisher's checksum file when it has network; a capture note is agent-written, so it is not evidence on its own (#301).\n421:  - when the rules cannot be read (a 5xx, a network error, a permissions 403), nothing merges that tick. Rule types ard does not evaluate (`workflows`, `code_scanning`, `required_deployments`) are logged at warn\n510:**`network` (#297, part of #276).** Every agent kind takes an optional boolean `network` override, default `true`. In this part it is advisory only and nothing is enforced: a role with `network: false` gets `ARD_NETWORK=0` (else `1`) in its job environment and a constant \"Network: unavailable.\" (else \"Network: available.\") line in the agent's user message. The value rides on the executor job and handle from config only, never from anything the agent can read or change. In host mode the controller logs that `network: false` is not enforced. `ard config check` prints `false (advisory)`, or `false (advisory, host)` for a host-mode role, and warns when a host-mode role that reads untrusted input has `network: true`, naming the GitHub App private key file as reachable.\n512:What `true` and `false` mean per executor, as the code does today: the host executor only sets `ARD_NETWORK` and the prompt line, and logs that `false` is not enforced; the Docker executor passes the same variable and prompt line and adds no network option to `docker run`, so the container has the default bridge network either way. Nothing blocks egress for either value, so `false` is a request the agent may ignore, not a boundary. Risks of a role with network (#276): (1) exfiltration of anything the agent can read, including its model credential and the checked-out source; (2) fetched content that carries prompt injection; (3) fetched bytes that change between runs, so results do not reproduce; (4) a compromised or typosquatted source; (5) fetching code or data that lands in the repository unreviewed; (6) a download that is large or slow and burns the job's time and spend; (7) a request to an internal or metadata address from the host or cluster. The residual risk is that none of these is closed by the setting: the diff guard, the reviewers, the `fetched:` records (URL, hash and verifier for every landed input) and the missing GitHub token limit the damage, and a human reviews every PR. Fetched bytes are untrusted data, never instructions, and are not a reason to trust the source. For roles with network, use a dedicated workspace API key with a spend cap, so a stolen or abused key can cost only a bounded amount.\n526:- **Agents hold no GitHub token.** The executor does. The agent's environment has only its model credential: an API key or a subscription OAuth token. The local executor does not enforce egress (see `network` under Configuration; the chart's NetworkPolicy comment is #296). In Kubernetes (#54) job egress is DNS and the internet minus private ranges and 169.254.169.254 (the #276 posture), and `pre` holds only a read-only per-job token revoked after it ends.\n531:- **Residual risks (Kubernetes).** The model credential sits in a per-job Secret in etcd, so etcd encryption at rest is required. The tee'd stdout is agent-writable, so the agent can forge its usage figures, the same exposure as local Docker, accepted by the owner; the exit code still comes from the kubelet; revisit when split log streams are available. Pod egress reaches the whole internet, and a `network: false` pod still allows GitHub and model API CIDRs. Pod Security `restricted` relies on the cluster's admission.\nagent\nagentjob\nbotcheck\nbudget\ncli\ncloseout\ncommitter\nconfig\nconfigcmd\nconfigsrc\ncontroller\ncostblock\ncosts\ndigest\ndocscheck\neffects\nexecutor\ngithub\ngitref\ngitx\ninitcmd\nintake\njanitor\njobexec\njob