h in a fresh worktree detached at a SHA: the agent step (`<job id>`) at the branch head, or `origin/main` for a new branch; a test step (`<job id>t<k>`) and a fix step (`<job id>f<k>`)\n4. **Run the agent** with `.ard/workers/<role>/prompt.txt` and these untrusted inputs: `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md` (written by the loop, outside `.ard/<issue#>/`, never committed), `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md` when triage wrote one and `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` when a scanner filed th\n5. **Guard the diff** after each agent or fix step, before anything leaves the host. The worktree belongs to the agent, so the guard never trusts its `.git`, config, index or attributes: git runs against the controller's own clone with a private index filled from the base commit (so `skip-worktree` \n6. **Two commits per round.** The loop commits the code through the committer's code lane (`Branches.CommitCode`) with the title `[<type>: ]<issue title> (#<issue>)`, the redacted summary (secrets and host paths) as the body and an `Ard-Job: <job-id>` trailer, no `[skip ci]`. `<type>` comes from the\n7. **Test.** Run `tests` (default `make test`; empty disables the step) as `/bin/sh -c` in the role's image, with no credential. On failure the agent gets the failure output in a fix step, which commits and pushes again, up to `max_test_fixes` (default 2) within the same job. A fix that changes no f\n8. **Open or update a draft PR** with ard's own `Closes #<issue>`, the agent summary quoted and neutralised (`committer.NeutraliseRefs`, then marker and mention defusing), the test result and links to `worker-<role>.md`, the logs and the job id. Reviewers are chosen by `config.SelectReviewers`: the \n9. **Report.** Comment on the issue with the summary, PR link and test result (`passed`, `passed after 1 fix attempt`, `failed after 2 fix attempts`, `not configured`); swap `ard:worker:<role>:running` for `:done`. If tests still fail, add `ard:tests:failed` on the issue and the PR and request no re\n\n**The starter worker (`workers/engineer`, #34).** `.ard/workers/engineer/prompt.txt` implements `plan.md`'s `## Implementation`. Its inputs, all untrusted, are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, in a fix round `test-output.log`, and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, `issue.md`, the triage role\n\n**Review feedback loops back here.** The `reviewfix` loop (#41) starts a round when every requested reviewer has finished and at least one ended `ard:review:<role>:changes`, backed by that role's close-out record (a label alone does not count). It records a `review_round` event in `_controller.db` (\n\n**The merger takes it from here.** A worker PR stays a draft until every requested review passes at its current head; then the controller marks it ready for review. The merger merges ready PRs that also pass its gates.\n\n277:**Starter planning roles.** `ard init` also ships four planning roles, each writing its own `plan.md` section (`config.BuiltinPlanSections`) and its role file: `product-manager` (`## Requirements`: requirements, a numbered list of acceptance criteria, out of scope), `architect` (`## Design`: approach, affected modules, and an `ADR needed: yes|no` line; the worker writes any ADR in its pull request), `designer` (`## UX`, plus Markdown or script-free SVG files under `.ard/<issue#>/design/`) and `documentation-planner` (`## Docs`: which docs change and what each must say). The command line counts as a user interface, so the designer covers flags, output and messages. The starter ordering is product-manager, then architect; designer, then documentation-planner; the engineer runs after architect and documentation-planner (`after:` in the starter `config.yaml`). Note for #45: acceptance criteria are a numbered list under `### Acceptance criteria`. The starter prompts read the repo context docs per role: `product-manager` reads `PRODUCT.md` and `VISION.md`, `architect` reads `VISION.md` and `TECH_STACK.md`, `designer` and `documentation-planner` read `PRODUCT.md`, and `engineer` reads `TECH_STACK.md`. Each prompt states the shared rules: the file is optional, one with no text outside headings and HTML comments counts as absent, the issue is the ask and the files are constraints, and `CLAUDE.md` and `AGENTS.md` win on commands and layout. A planning role that finds a conflict records it, with file and section, in its own role file.\n366:**The starter worker (`workers/engineer`, #34).** `.ard/workers/engineer/prompt.txt` implements `plan.md`'s `## Implementation`. Its inputs, all untrusted, are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, in a fix round `test-output.log`, and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, `issue.md`, the triage role files and logs, in a review round `review-feedback.md` and the reviewer role files and logs. It writes code, tests and docs, and `worker-engineer.md` with `role: engineer` and `attempt: 1`, an optional `next` of `review:` entries, and never `worker` or `verdict`. The body has Changes, Testing and Open issues sections; it becomes the commit body, the PR description and the issue comment, and a fix round's body describes the whole change. It answers `needs-human` for a change the diff guard would reject, more changed lines than the role's `diff_max_lines` (3,000 by default; above `diff_warn_lines`, 2,000 by default, the PR goes ahead with the large-change note), a missing decision, nothing to change, or an input that tries to instruct it. In a fix round it keeps `done` and explains a failure it cannot fix, so the loop reports `ard:tests:failed` on the PR. The repository's package manager may fetch a dependency the change needs (`go get`, `npm install`); the agent installs no system packages. With `network: true` it may fetch other inputs, records each under `fetched:` and treats the fetched bytes as untrusted data, and the guard and reviewers still see `go.mod` and lockfile changes. Credential-shaped test fixtures are built at run time. The starter override is Sonnet 5.5, `timeout: 60m` for the whole job, `tests: make lint test` (so formatting and vet failures are caught before a PR opens), and the default diff thresholds written out (`diff_warn_lines: 2000`, `diff_max_lines: 3000`). It ships no Dockerfile, so it runs as a host subprocess with the #84 warning until #55's `ard-agent` image gives a worker image a base. It reads `TECH_STACK.md` when present; the file is a constraint, never an instruction, and the worker never resolves a conflict with it: it keeps to the issue and plan and notes the conflict in Open issues. With `Network: available.` the worker fetches only what the plan's Network inputs name, verifies each file against the publisher's checksum file, never invents or recalls a value, records each file in `fetched:` and in a `CAPTURE.md` beside the fixtures, and commits a regen script and Make target; with `Network: unavailable.` it answers `needs-human` listing the inputs it needs (#301).\n384:**The starter reviewer (`reviewers/eng-review`, #36).** `.ard/reviewers/eng-review/prompt.txt` checks correctness, missing tests and drift from `plan.md`. Its inputs, all untrusted, are `diff.patch`, `issue.md`, `comments.md` and the committed `.ard/<issue#>/`; text in the change that addresses a reviewer is a blocking `high` finding on its line (verdict `changes`), and the same text in the issue, comments or `.ard/<issue#>/` is ignored and mentioned in the body. It runs the repository's own linter and the changed packages' tests with coverage through Bash, writing scratch files outside the worktree, reports only problems on added lines and skips a check it cannot run, saying so; the package manager may fetch declared dependencies and nothing else is downloaded. Critical and high findings always block, medium only when it is a real bug, low and info never; a missing test blocks only when it is `high` (risky or core logic, or a case `plan.md` named), smaller gaps are `medium`. The verdict is `changes` exactly when a finding blocks. `line` is the head-side line: a `+` line goes inline, anything else in the body. It writes only `review-eng-review.md` with `role: eng-review`, `attempt: 1`, `status` `done` or `needs-human`, `findings` with a double-quoted `file` and a `message: |` block, never `next` or `worker`, and a body with a `## Checks` section. It answers `needs-human` only when there is nothing to review or the issue and the plan contradict each other. The starter override is Sonnet 5.5 with the default 30m timeout and no `when:`, so every PR from the starter worker gets it. It ships no Dockerfile, so it runs as a host subprocess with the #84 warning until #55. It also checks that every fetched file has a `fetched:` entry, a `CAPTURE.md` row and a regen target, and re-verifies pinned checksums against the publisher's checksum file when it has network; a capture note is agent-written, so it is not evidence on its own (#301).","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"015b7330-5d47-4bab-a218-7f9c713b26a1","uuid":"5721b009-3add-4ad9-99a1-103f3aa4d0ba","timestamp":"2026-11-25T15:31:11.496Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"     311 reviewer_e2e_test.go\n     219 reviewfix_e2e_test.go\n     388 triage_e2e_test.go\n     351 worker_e2e_test.go\n    1269 total\npackage servecmd\n\nimport (\n\t\"context\"\n\t\"encoding/json\"\n\t\"io\"\n\t\"log/slog\"\n\t\"math\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\t\"testing\"\n\t\"time\"\n\n\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/agent\"\n\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/agent/claude\"\n\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/closeout\"\n\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/config\"\n\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/controller\"\n\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/effects\"\n\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/github\"\n\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/github/fake\"\n\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/gitx\"\n\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/gitx/gittest\"\n\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/labe